  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Tyler Mahle gave them seven sharp innings, and the sixth inning decided the game.",
    "what_this_means": "Casey Schmitt's home run in the sixth put them ahead for good, and that clean arm out of Mahle kept Colorado quiet long enough for it to stick. The offense was sparse. Seven hits, nine strikeouts. But the two runs they scored with runners in scoring position were enough. JT Brubaker closed the final two innings without damage. This is what managing narrow leads looks like: pitching that doesn't crack, timely hitting when it matters, and a bullpen that doesn't leak. They won by two runs at home against a team 20.5 games back. The margin held because the formula held.",
    "what_to_watch": "Michael Lorenzen brings a 6.46 ERA into tonight's rematch against Trevor McDonald. The Colorado offense has not adjusted to early-inning pressure. They've left runners stranded in four straight games. If McDonald can match Mahle's length, the Giants have a real chance to build on the foundation of the last two days.",
